# ============================ SYSADMIN 5 ============================
LESSONS_S5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Every change is staged, documented, reversible", "11", "<p>This module covers changes to the system itself: platform updates from the host's stream, the test environment, the customisation layers from Customize Form to code, the customisation register, the change procedure, and the smoke test.</p><p><b>Why change is its own module.</b> Every other module governs data inside a stable system. This one governs changes <b>to the system</b> — new versions, new fields, changed forms, new logic. Change is where stable systems break, and the breakage has a signature: it lands on the busiest doctype, at the busiest hour, and its cause is whatever nobody wrote down. The discipline is three words, and every chapter serves them:</p><p>• <b>Staged</b> — every change runs in the test environment before it runs in production. No exceptions for \"small\" changes: the field relabel that hides a mandatory field breaks posting exactly as thoroughly as a code change.<br>• <b>Documented</b> — every change has a register entry: what, why, who owns it, when, and the test evidence. Chapter 5's register is the module's central artifact.<br>• <b>Reversible</b> — every change deploys behind a before-change backup (module 4's rule, applied to structure as well as data) and with its undo path named in advance.</p><p><b>The two change streams.</b></p><p>1. <b>The host's stream — updates.</b> CloudERP.One updates the platform and the apps: security patches, fixes, version upgrades. The sysadmin does not write these but <b>receives them governed</b>: release notes read, the test site updated first, the smoke test run, the window scheduled. Chapter 2.<br>2. <b>The site's stream — customisations.</b> The business's own changes: form adjustments, custom fields, notifications, workflows, and — governed hardest — scripts. Chapters 3-5.</p><p><b>The standing tension, named honestly.</b> The business wants changes fast; the discipline adds a day. The answer is not to argue the discipline down but to make its fast lane genuinely fast: a Customize Form change staged, signed, and deployed inside one day is the standard this module builds — slow governance breeds cowboy changes exactly as module 3's slow request lane breeds duplicate masters.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the host's update stream, received governed. Chapter 3: the test environment. Chapter 4: the customisation layers — Customize Form, Custom Fields, and code. Chapter 5: the customisation register. Chapter 6: the change procedure end to end. Chapter 7: the smoke test. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The update stream, received governed", "12", "<p>The host maintains the platform: security patches, bug fixes, and version upgrades arrive on CloudERP.One's schedule. The sysadmin's job is receiving them so the site never learns by surprise.</p><p><b>The update kinds, by weight.</b></p><p>• <b>Security patches</b> — applied promptly by the host, sometimes without a scheduling conversation; the sysadmin's job is the after-check (the smoke test's short form) and staying informed.<br>• <b>Maintenance releases</b> — fixes within the current version; low risk, still received governed: notes read, test first where the window allows.<br>• <b>Version upgrades</b> — the heavy events: new major versions change behaviour, layouts, and occasionally the ground under customisations. These are scheduled like the projects they are.</p><p><b>The receiving procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Read the release notes against the site.</b> The host announces what changes; the sysadmin reads with the module map open (module 1) and the customisation register beside it (Chapter 5): which of our modules are touched, which of our customisations sit on ground that is moving. Ten minutes that convert an announcement into an impact list.<br>2. <b>Update the test site first.</b> The standing arrangement with the host: the test environment receives the update ahead of production. The smoke test (Chapter 7) runs there, plus targeted checks on the impact list.<br>3. <b>Schedule the production window.</b> Off-peak for the business — never the close week, never month-end filing days; the calendar (module 1) marks the standing no-update windows. Staff are told: when, how long, what to expect.<br>4. <b>The before-change backup</b> — the site's own on-demand set, timestamped in the log, belt to the host's braces (module 4's rule).<br>5. <b>The update runs (the host's hands), then the smoke test runs (the sysadmin's).</b> Chapter 7's checklist, immediately, before staff resume — findings raised on the spot while the window is still open.<br>6. <b>Log and announce.</b> The version change recorded (the About page's new numbers into the register), the all-clear sent, anything user-visible noted in one line.</p><p><b>The deferral question.</b> Can updates be postponed? Maintenance and features, within reason and by agreement. Security patches, no — a known vulnerability on a system holding the books is not a scheduling preference. The written update policy states both halves so the conversation never happens under pressure.</p><p>Practical rule: the update that has never caused a problem is the one that breeds the skipped smoke test — and the skipped smoke test is how the print-format break trades quietly for three days until a customer receives a broken invoice. Run the checklist every time; it is twelve minutes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The test environment", "12", "<p>The test site is the module's load-bearing infrastructure: a second site, provisioned by the host, that exists to absorb risk production must never see.</p><p><b>What runs there — the standing list.</b></p><p>• <b>Restore drills</b> (module 4's monthly line — the drill and this chapter share the site).<br>• <b>Update previews</b> (Chapter 2's step 2).<br>• <b>Customisation builds and sign-offs</b> (Chapters 4-6): every change is built here, and the requesting owner approves it <b>here</b>, on realistic data, before production hears of it.<br>• <b>Import slices</b> (module 3's ten-row dry runs).<br>• <b>Training and experiments:</b> the new cashier's practice sessions, the \"what happens if\" questions — answered here where the answer costs nothing.</p><p><b>The refresh cycle.</b> A test site drifts from production — data ages, settings diverge — and a stale test site gives stale answers: the customisation that worked in test against last year's data meets this year's volumes differently. The procedure: <b>refresh the test site from a production backup on a schedule</b> (monthly rides naturally on the restore drill — the drill's restore <i>is</i> the refresh) and always before a heavy piece of work (a version-upgrade preview, a big import rehearsal). The refresh is logged like the backups it comes from.</p><p><b>The two rules that keep the test site honest.</b></p><p>1. <b>Test data discipline.</b> The refreshed test site contains real business data — the same customers, salaries, and prices as production — so it is protected by the same access design: module 2's permissions apply, test-site accounts are real named accounts, and the leaver procedure covers both sites. A test site with an open door is a production data breach with extra steps.<br>2. <b>Unmistakable identity.</b> The test site must announce itself — the site name distinct, and where the platform supports it, a visual banner — because the standing accident is the document entered into the wrong site: the real invoice typed into test (lost work) or the test experiment typed into production (a correction case). Every training session opens by pointing at the identity markers.</p><p><b>What test does not prove.</b> Honest limits, stated once: the test site proves function, not scale (production's concurrent load and full history behave differently at the margins), and proves the change, not the users (the front line will find the path no tester walked). The smoke test after production deployment (Chapter 7) exists because test passing is necessary, never sufficient.</p><p>Practical rule: the test site's health is a monthly calendar line — reachable, refreshed on schedule, access list current. A dead test site quietly converts every future change into test-in-production, which is Chapter 9's first pattern.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The customisation layers", "13", "<p>Site customisations come in layers, ordered by power and therefore by governance weight. The standing rule: <b>solve at the lowest layer that solves it.</b></p><p><b>Layer 1 — Customize Form.</b> Open <b>Customize Form</b> (awesome bar), select the doctype, and adjust its presentation and behaviour without code:</p><p>• Relabel fields to the business's language; reorder; hide fields the business never uses (a hidden field simplifies every day at that keyboard).<br>• Set fields <b>mandatory</b> (module 3's completeness enforcement lives here), set defaults, set read-only.<br>• Adjust list settings and search fields.</p><p>Customize Form changes are stored as overrides on top of the standard doctype — they <b>survive updates</b> and can be reverted per property. This is the workhorse layer: most business requests (\"can this field be required\", \"can we hide that section\") end here, and its fast lane should run inside a day. Two cautions carry its whole risk: hiding or requiring a field affects <b>every role that touches the form</b> (impact analysis is still real), and mandatory-with-history means old incomplete records now block on edit — decided knowingly.</p><p><b>Layer 2 — Custom Fields.</b> Adding fields the business needs that the standard form lacks — the delivery route on an invoice, the licence number on a customer. Created through Customize Form (or the Custom Field doctype directly), with the discipline:</p><p>• <b>Named to be recognisable as custom</b> (the platform prefixes internally; the label and description state the business meaning).<br>• <b>Typed correctly at birth</b> — a Link to a proper master beats free text every time it will be filtered or reported on (a free-text \"route\" field yields seventeen spellings of one route; a Link to a Route master yields a report).<br>• <b>Positioned deliberately</b> in the form's flow, and added to list/print views where it earns it.<br>• <b>Registered</b> — every custom field is a register entry (Chapter 5) with an owner and a purpose. The field nobody can explain is this module's version of the account nobody can explain.</p><p>Custom fields survive updates; their <b>meaning</b> is what decays without the register.</p><p><b>Layer 3 — logic: Notifications, Workflows, and scripts.</b> Notifications (module 6) and Workflows (module 2) are configuration-level logic — governed, but codeless. <b>Client and Server Scripts</b> are the code layer: real programming inside the site, capable of anything and therefore governed hardest — specified in writing, built and tested in the test site, reviewed, registered with an owner, and — the platform reality — server-side code on managed hosting may be restricted or routed through the host's review, which is a feature: the constraint is the governance. The rule that keeps this layer small: <b>a script is the last resort after Customize Form, a custom field, a Notification, and a Workflow have each been ruled out in writing.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The customisation register", "12", "<p>The register is one page per customisation, and the collection is the site's memory of how it differs from standard. It is the module's central artifact because every other discipline depends on it: update impact analysis reads it, the change procedure writes it, and the annual reconciliation proves it.</p><p><b>The entry, per customisation.</b></p><p>1. <b>What</b> — the object, precisely: the doctype, the field/property/script name, the layer (Customize Form / Custom Field / Notification / Workflow / Script).<br>2. <b>Why</b> — the business purpose in one sentence, in the owner's words. This line is the one that saves the next sysadmin.<br>3. <b>Who</b> — the requesting owner (the person who answers \"do we still need this\") and the builder.<br>4. <b>When</b> — requested, tested, deployed — with the test sign-off and the before-change backup timestamp.<br>5. <b>The undo path</b> — how this comes out cleanly if retired: revert the property, hide-don't-delete the field (a custom field with data is retired by hiding, never deleting — the data is history), disable the notification, version the script.</p><p><b>Why the register earns its keep, three ways.</b></p><p>• <b>Update safety:</b> Chapter 2's step 1 reads release notes <b>against the register</b> — \"the invoice form is changing in this version; we have three customisations on it\" is a sentence only a register can produce.<br>• <b>The explanation debt:</b> without the register, every field accumulates the question \"what does this do, who wanted it, can we remove it\" — and the safe answer to unexplained structure is always \"leave it\", which is how sites fossilise. With the register, retirement is routine.<br>• <b>Continuity:</b> the register is the customisation chapter of module 4's handover — a new sysadmin reads it and knows the site.</p><p><b>The annual reconciliation.</b> Once a year (the calendar's customisation line): the site's actual customisations are listed from the system's own records — the Customize Form overrides, the Custom Field list, the Notification and Workflow lists, the script list — and reconciled against the register. Three findings and their handling:</p><p>1. <b>On the site, not in the register</b> — the undocumented change: registered retroactively with its archaeology, and the gap in the change procedure that admitted it is fixed.<br>2. <b>In the register, not on the site</b> — the ghost entry: closed with a note.<br>3. <b>Registered but unowned</b> — the owner left; the entry is re-homed or the customisation is nominated for retirement at the owner review: each owner re-confirms annually that their customisations still earn their place. Sites accrete; the review is the pruning.</p><p>Practical rule: the register's quality test is the stranger test — could a competent stranger, given only the register, explain every difference between this site and a standard install? Module 4's handover test, applied to structure.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The change procedure, end to end", "12", "<p>One procedure covers every site-stream change, scaled by weight. Eight steps; the light version runs inside a day.</p><p><b>1. The request, written.</b> Who wants it, what outcome, why — in the requester's words. \"Add a field\" is not a request; \"we need to record the delivery route on invoices so dispatch can plan\" is. The outcome framing matters because step 2 may find a lower-layer answer than the requester imagined.</p><p><b>2. Impact analysis, with the maps.</b> The module map (who works in this doctype), the register (what already customises it), and the layer question (Chapter 4's lowest-layer rule). Output: the chosen layer, the affected roles and prints and reports, and the undo path — three sentences for a small change, a page for a script.</p><p><b>3. Build in test.</b> On the refreshed test site, never production. For Customize Form work this is minutes; the discipline is the venue, not the effort.</p><p><b>4. Owner sign-off in test.</b> The requesting owner exercises the change on the test site against realistic data and approves in writing (a message suffices; it goes in the register entry). This step kills the commonest failure — the change that technically matches the request and practically misses the need — while it is still free.</p><p><b>5. The before-change backup.</b> Module 4's rule: the on-demand set, timestamp into the log line. For structure changes as for data.</p><p><b>6. Deploy, in a sane window.</b> Small changes: any quiet moment, announced where user-visible. Anything touching posting paths or many roles: the same off-peak discipline as updates. Never the close week.</p><p><b>7. Verify live.</b> The smoke test's targeted form: the changed path exercised in production immediately — the field appears, the mandatory enforces, the notification fires — plus one standard document through the touched doctype (the posting map check where posting is anywhere near the change).</p><p><b>8. Register and announce.</b> The register entry completed (Chapter 5's five parts), and the one-line announcement to the affected roles: what changed, what it looks like, who to tell if something is off. An unannounced change generates a week of \"is this broken?\" tickets that one sentence prevents.</p><p><b>Scaling by weight.</b> The steps never drop; their depth scales. A relabel: steps 1-2 are two sentences, 3-4 ten minutes, the whole lane a morning. A script: every step is real work and the timeline is honest about it. What never happens at any weight: skipping test (\"it's tiny\"), skipping the owner (\"it's obvious\"), skipping the register (\"I'll remember\") — Chapter 9's patterns are exactly these three skips, aged.</p><p>Practical rule: pre-print the eight steps as the change ticket's template. A procedure that lives in the ticket gets followed; one that lives in a manual gets remembered selectively.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The smoke test", "12", "<p>After every update and every significant deployment, the smoke test proves the site's critical paths before staff resume. It is a written checklist, owned, timed at roughly twelve minutes, and its logic is the posting map exercised live.</p><p><b>The standing checklist.</b></p><p>1. <b>Login and Desk</b> — a standard (non-admin) account logs in; the workspaces render; the awesome bar answers.<br>2. <b>The trading path</b> — one test Sales Invoice: created, submitted, <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger read</b> (the rows match the posting map), printed to PDF (the print path proves the format and letterhead survived — the classic silent casualty of updates), then cancelled clean.<br>3. <b>The stock path</b> — one Stock Entry or Delivery Note cycle where stock is live: submit, ledger read, cancel.<br>4. <b>The payment path</b> — one Payment Entry against the test invoice before its cancellation: references resolve, the bank leaf posts.<br>5. <b>The reports</b> — the Trial Balance and one standard report per live module open and render with plausible figures.<br>6. <b>The channels</b> — one email sends from the site and arrives (the queue moves — module 6); one Notification fires where quickly testable.<br>7. <b>The customisation spot-checks</b> — from the register: the custom fields visible, the key Customize Form properties holding (the mandatory still mandatory), any script's happy path exercised. The register makes this step a list, not a memory exercise.<br>8. <b>The integrations</b> — each registered integration's health check (module 6's register defines it; for the e-invoicing rail, the check the tax module relies on).</p><p><b>The rules around it.</b></p><p>• <b>Run before staff resume</b>, inside the window — a finding raised while the host's hands are still on the update is a fix; the same finding on Thursday is an incident with three days of consequences.<br>• <b>Documents are test-marked and cleaned:</b> the test invoice is cancelled, the test entries reversed — the smoke test must not leave droppings in the books (use the standing test customer/item so even the residue is recognisable).<br>• <b>Log the run:</b> date, trigger (which update/change), checks passed, minutes, by whom — one line beside the update's register entry. Findings get the incident treatment: logged, raised or fixed, and — where the smoke test itself missed something that surfaced later — <b>the checklist is amended</b>, which is how the list earns its shape over the years.</p><p>Practical rule: the smoke test is deliberately shallow and wide — every critical path touched once, nothing exhaustively. Depth belongs in the test site before deployment; the smoke test only proves the deployment landed. Twelve minutes, every time, no exceptions for boring updates: the boring update that breaks printing is this module's oldest story.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's change year", "13", "<p>The change discipline, installed and exercised. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The ground.</b> The site carries the implementation era's accumulation: customisations nobody listed, a test site that exists but was last refreshed \"sometime last year\", and update history in the host's records only. The first annual reconciliation (Chapter 5) is run retroactively: the site's actual overrides, custom fields, notifications, and workflows are listed from the system's own records — <b>14 customisations</b> found, of which <b>9</b> are explained and registered with owners, and <b>5</b> defy explanation. The five get the archaeology: two turn out to be abandoned experiments (retired — the custom fields hidden, not deleted, per the undo rule), one is a duplicate of standard behaviour (retired), and two are quietly load-bearing (registered with their reconstructed purpose and new owners). The register exists by month's end; the stranger test passes.</p><p><b>The test site brought honest.</b> The refresh cycle is tied to the restore drill (module 4's monthly restore <b>is</b> the refresh), the identity banner is confirmed unmistakable after one near-miss in training week (a practice invoice nearly typed into production — the trainer's story becomes the standing example), and the access list is aligned with module 2's matrix.</p><p><b>A change through the lane (Chapters 4, 6).</b> Dispatch requests delivery-route tracking on invoices. The lane runs as designed: the written request names the outcome; impact analysis chooses <b>Layer 2</b> — a custom <b>Route</b> master (a tiny doctype) and a Link field on the Sales Invoice, explicitly rejecting the free-text shortcut (\"seventeen spellings of one route yields no report\"); built in test in an afternoon; the dispatch owner signs off in test after one adjustment (the field moved next to the shipping address, where the eye goes); before-change backup at 07:14; deployed in the morning quiet; verified live (field present, link resolves, one test invoice through the posting map); registered; announced in one line. Elapsed: two working days, most of it the owner's sign-off latency. The fast lane earns its reputation, and the next three requests arrive <b>through</b> the lane rather than around it.</p><p><b>An update cycle (Chapters 2, 7).</b> The host announces a maintenance release. The notes are read against the register (one touched area: printing). The test site updates first; the targeted check passes there — but the production smoke test's step 2 catches it anyway: the invoice PDF renders with the letterhead displaced. Raised inside the window with the exact reproduction; the host applies the fix before staff resume; the smoke-test log's line reads <i>finding raised and closed in-window</i>. The checklist's print step, already the list's veteran, keeps its seat. The MD's note for the quarter: <i>the system now changes the way the books close — on a procedure, with evidence, and nothing by surprise.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "11", "<p>The change layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — test-in-production.</b> Symptom: experiments run live; \"let's just try it\" on the real books; the correction traffic that follows. Cause: no test site, or a dead one (Chapter 3). Fix: the test site as standing infrastructure — refreshed on the drill's rhythm, identity unmistakable, health on the calendar — and the venue rule absolute: every change builds in test, at every weight.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the undocumented site.</b> Symptom: fields nobody can explain; the \"what does this do\" question answered with \"leave it\"; a site that fossilises because removal is riskier than accretion. Cause: changes without register entries (Chapter 5). Fix: the retroactive reconciliation once — archaeology, ownership, retirements — then the register as step 8 of every change, and the annual owner review pruning what no longer earns its place.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the surprise update.</b> Symptom: the platform changes and the business discovers it from breakage; printing dies on a Tuesday; nobody read the notes. Cause: the host's stream received ungoverned (Chapter 2). Fix: the receiving procedure — notes against the register, test first, scheduled window, before-change backup, smoke test before staff resume — and the update policy's written line on what may be deferred and what may not.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the script reflex.</b> Symptom: code as the first answer; a thicket of scripts doing what Customize Form, a custom field, a Notification, or a Workflow would do; every update a minefield. Cause: the lowest-layer rule unknown or unenforced (Chapter 4). Fix: the layer ladder applied in writing at impact analysis, the script bar set high (last resort, specified, reviewed, owned), and the thicket thinned at the annual review.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the skipped smoke test.</b> Symptom: \"the update was boring\" — and the break trades quietly for days until a customer or the close finds it. Cause: the checklist treated as optional for routine events (Chapter 7). Fix: the twelve minutes every time, the run logged beside the update entry, and the checklist amended by every miss so it compounds instead of decaying.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the free-text field.</b> Symptom: a custom field filled seventeen ways; reports impossible; the cleanup import annual. Cause: typed wrong at birth (Chapter 4). Fix: the Link-to-master discipline for anything that will be filtered or reported, and the retype done once, properly — a small master, a governed migration of values, the register entry updated.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the three-word discipline and the two change streams; run the update receiving procedure including the deferral policy's two halves; keep the test site honest — refresh rhythm, data discipline, unmistakable identity — and state what test does not prove; walk the layer ladder from Customize Form through Custom Fields to scripts with the lowest-layer rule; write a five-part register entry and run the annual reconciliation's three findings; execute the eight-step change procedure scaled by weight; and run the smoke test's eight checks with its rules.</p><p><b>The fifth law: every change is staged in test, signed by its owner, deployed behind a backup, verified live, and written in the register — the site never learns by surprise.</b> Module 6 covers the channels: printing, email, and integrations.</p>"),
]

