# ============================ SYSADMIN 6 ============================
LESSONS_S6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The channels: every one owned", "11", "<p>This module covers the system's faces to the world: printed documents, email, and integrations. Each is a <b>channel</b> — a path by which the system speaks or connects — and the module's design is one rule applied three ways: <b>every channel has an owner, an identity the business controls, credentials under custody, and a health read on the calendar.</b></p><p><b>Why channels get a module.</b> Channels fail differently from data. A data error is wrong inside the system, found by the system's own checks. A channel failure is <b>silence or embarrassment outside</b> the system:</p><p>• The email queue stalls, and the statements Process Statement Of Accounts faithfully generated arrive nowhere — collections weakens for a month before anyone connects the dots.<br>• The invoice prints with a stretched logo and a missing tax line — every customer receives the business's carelessness in PDF form.<br>• The e-invoicing integration's credential expires, and the compliance rail the tax module depends on goes quiet.</p><p>None of these announce themselves in a list view. The module's machinery — the queue read, the print standards, the integration register with its health checks — exists to make channel failures loud and fast instead of quiet and slow.</p><p><b>The channel inventory.</b> The sysadmin keeps one page listing every channel:</p><p>1. <b>Print</b> — the document formats and the letterhead (Chapters 2-3).<br>2. <b>Email out</b> — the sending identity, the queue, the automated senders: Notifications, Auto Email Reports, the statement run (Chapters 4-6).<br>3. <b>Email in</b> — where inbound mail creates or updates records, where configured.<br>4. <b>Integrations</b> — every external system connected: the e-invoicing rail (NGE Portal), payment or SMS gateways where used, and anything with an API key (Chapters 7-8).</p><p>Per channel: the owner, the identity (domain, sender address, credential), where the credential lives, the health check, and the known failure mode. This inventory is the module's register, and module 1's monthly integration line reads from it — a channel missing from the inventory is a channel whose failure will be diagnosed from zero, at the worst time.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: Letter Head and Print Settings. Chapter 3: Print Formats. Chapter 4: Email Accounts — the sending identity. Chapter 5: the Email Queue. Chapter 6: Notifications and Auto Email Reports. Chapter 7: integrations and credential custody. Chapter 8: the integration register and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Letter Head and Print Settings", "12", "<p>Every printed document carries the business's face before it carries its figures. Two doctypes set the standard once.</p><p><b>The Letter Head doctype.</b> Open <b>Letter Head</b> (awesome bar): the header (and optionally footer) that rides every printed document — the logo, the legal name, addresses, registration and tax identifiers, contacts. The rules:</p><p>• <b>Content is a policy decision, not a design whim:</b> what must legally appear on an invoice (registration numbers, tax identity) is confirmed with the accountant; the letterhead is where it lives once so no format forgets it.<br>• <b>One default, marked:</b> the default letterhead applies everywhere automatically; additional letterheads exist only for real cases (a second company on the site gets its own — the multi-company rule reaching into print; a branch variant only if genuinely needed).<br>• <b>The image discipline:</b> the logo uploaded at proper resolution, sized in the letterhead itself, and tested to PDF before the letterhead ships — the stretched-logo invoice is this chapter's classic failure, and it is a two-minute test.<br>• <b>Changes are governed events:</b> a letterhead change alters every document printed from that day — module 5's change lane applies (test site, sign-off, register), and old printed documents in circulation mean the change date is worth logging.</p><p><b>Print Settings.</b> Open <b>Print Settings</b>: the site-wide print policy page, walked once like every settings page:</p><p>• The PDF page size (A4 for Nigerian business correspondence), fonts and text sizing where exposed.<br>• <b>Letterhead-by-default</b> on, so nothing prints naked.<br>• Currency and amount formatting confirmed against System Settings' precision decisions (module 1) — the amount that displays differently on screen and paper is a settings mismatch, not a bug.<br>• The print server / PDF generation options as provided by the platform, noted in the walk record.</p><p><b>The standing test.</b> After any change to letterhead, print settings, or formats — and inside every smoke test (module 5's step 2) — one document of each critical kind is printed to PDF and <b>read like a customer would</b>: logo clean, identity complete, figures aligned, totals and tax lines present, footer intact. The PDF is the product here; the screen preview approximates it but the PDF is what leaves the building.</p><p>Practical rule: collect one signed-off specimen PDF per critical document (invoice, statement, receipt, purchase order) in the channel register. The specimen is the standard the smoke test compares against — \"does it still look like this\" is a faster and stricter test than \"does it look right\".</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Print Formats", "12", "<p>The <b>Print Format</b> decides how each doctype renders on paper. The letterhead is the frame; the format is the body.</p><p><b>The three levels, by governance weight (module 5's layer ladder, applied to print).</b></p><p>1. <b>The standard formats</b> — every doctype ships with one. Adequate for internal documents; the default starting point.<br>2. <b>The Print Format Builder</b> — the codeless editor: open a doctype's print view, choose to customise, and arrange fields, columns, and sections by drag and configuration. This is the workhorse for business-facing documents: the invoice showing the fields <b>this</b> business's customers need (the custom Route field from module 5 earning its print placement here), columns ordered for readability, the remarks section where signatures go.<br>3. <b>Custom HTML formats</b> — code-level control for pixel-specific needs. Module 5's script rules apply verbatim: last resort, specified, tested in the test site, registered with an owner — and every platform update's smoke test exercises them, because code-level formats are the update's favourite casualty.</p><p><b>The assignment discipline.</b> Each doctype has a <b>default format</b>; users with print access can often choose among available formats at print time. Governance:</p><p>• The default per critical doctype is a <b>decision in the channel register</b> — the invoice's default is the signed-off specimen's format, full stop.<br>• Redundant and draft formats are cleaned up or clearly named (<i>DRAFT — do not use</i> beats a menu of five plausible invoices).<br>• Where a real second format exists (a delivery-note variant without prices for drivers, a proforma), it is named for its purpose and listed in the register with its use case.</p><p><b>Format content rules, carried from the tracks.</b> The programme's modules have already decided much of what must print; the sysadmin implements:</p><p>• The invoice: the tax lines and identifiers the tax module requires, the payment terms and bank details the receivables routine relies on (details from the <b>system's</b> master records — never typed into the format, where they fossilise).<br>• The statement: the format Process Statement Of Accounts sends — tested by sending one to a test address and reading it as the customer.<br>• The receipt and the POS print: the POS track's till formats, referenced here for completeness.</p><p>Practical rule: fields print from the document; identity prints from the letterhead; nothing prints from text hard-typed into a format. The hard-typed bank account that survived three real account changes is a known way businesses direct customer payments to a closed account — the channel register's specimen review catches it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Email Accounts: the sending identity", "12", "<p>Open <b>Email Account</b> (awesome bar). Every email the site sends or receives runs through an Email Account record, and the identity question is settled here.</p><p><b>The outgoing account.</b> The record carries the sender address, the mail service's connection settings (as the platform and the business's mail provider require), and the flags that matter:</p><p>• <b>Enable Outgoing</b> — this account may send.<br>• <b>Default Outgoing</b> — the site's standing sender: transaction emails, statements, notifications all leave under this identity unless a specific account overrides.</p><p><b>The identity rule — the chapter's core.</b> The sending address is an address <b>at the business's own domain</b> (accounts@ the business's domain), never a personal mailbox and never a free-mail address. Three reasons, each sufficient:</p><p>1. <b>Deliverability:</b> business mail systems increasingly distrust free-mail senders for commercial documents; the domain identity with the provider's proper authentication is what lands statements in inboxes rather than spam folders. The deliverability setup (the domain's mail authentication records) is done once with the domain's custodian — the difference between the statement run working and silently decorating spam folders.<br>2. <b>Continuity:</b> a personal mailbox leaves with its person — the leaver procedure cannot chase it, and the sent history goes with them.<br>3. <b>Identity:</b> the customer's reply, the dispute, the audit trail — all anchored to an address the business controls.</p><p><b>The credential custody.</b> The mail account's password/app-credential is an integration credential (Chapter 7's rules apply): held in the business's credential custody, not in one person's head; rotated on the security calendar and at relevant leavers; and — the operational note that saves a future incident — <b>rotation includes updating the Email Account record</b>, because the rotated password that never reached the site is the stuck-queue incident of Chapter 5 waiting to happen.</p><p><b>Incoming accounts, where used.</b> An Email Account can also receive — <b>Enable Incoming</b> — and file inbound mail against a doctype (the support-desk pattern: mail to support@ creating Issue records). Where the business uses this: the same identity and custody rules, plus a named owner for the receiving doctype's queue, because an inbound channel nobody reads is worse than none — senders believe they have been heard.</p><p>Practical rule: send the identity test after any mail change — one email from the site to an external mailbox at a major provider, and confirm it arrives in the <b>inbox</b>, sender displayed correctly. Thirty seconds; it is the difference between configured and working.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The Email Queue", "12", "<p>Open <b>Email Queue</b> (awesome bar). Every outbound email the site generates passes through this list on its way out, and the queue is the channel's health instrument.</p><p><b>Reading the queue.</b> Each row is one email with its status:</p><p>• <b>Not Sent</b> — generated, awaiting dispatch. A handful, briefly, is normal traffic; a growing pile is the alarm.<br>• <b>Sending / Sent</b> — in flight / delivered to the mail service.<br>• <b>Error</b> — dispatch failed, with the error recorded on the row. The error text is the diagnosis: authentication failures point at credentials (Chapter 4's rotation note), connection failures at the mail service or its settings, rejection messages at the recipient side or deliverability.</p><p><b>The daily glance (module 1's calendar line).</b> Thirty seconds: the queue's recent rows scanned for Error status and for a Not Sent pile that is not draining. The stakes justify the ritual: the queue carries <b>business documents</b> — invoices to customers, statements from the monthly run, purchase orders to suppliers, the compliance calendar's Notification reminders. A stuck queue is all of those silently not arriving, and nothing else in the system will mention it.</p><p><b>The stuck-queue procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Read the newest errors</b> — the error text names the family (credential, connection, rejection).<br>2. <b>Fix the cause:</b> the rotated-but-not-updated password corrected on the Email Account record; the mail-service settings re-verified; the deliverability problem escalated to the domain custodian.<br>3. <b>Confirm the drain:</b> queued mail retries; watch Not Sent convert to Sent. Where rows aged beyond usefulness (the reminder for a meeting past), the business decision is made deliberately: send late or cancel, and for customer-facing documents late is almost always right — the invoice must arrive.<br>4. <b>The identity test</b> (Chapter 4) as the closing check.<br>5. <b>Log the incident</b> with cause and duration — a queue that sticks twice for the same cause is a process fault (the rotation procedure missing its update step, usually).</p><p><b>The volume read.</b> Beside errors, the queue's volume tells stories: the statement run's monthly spike confirms it fired (the absence of the spike is the finding); an unexplained surge points at a misconfigured Notification looping (Chapter 6's standing caution); near-zero traffic on a site that should hum means senders are failing upstream.</p><p>Practical rule: after any mail-related change — credentials, Email Account settings, the provider's side — the queue is watched through one natural send. Configured is not working; the queue draining is working.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Notifications and Auto Email Reports", "13", "<p>Two doctypes turn the email channel into the programme's reminder and reporting machinery — the \"set system Notifications against the dates\" line that every track's calendar has been assuming is built here.</p><p><b>The Notification doctype.</b> Open <b>Notification</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New. Each record is one automated message rule:</p><p>• <b>The document type and event</b> — on save, on submit, on cancel, on a value change, or — the calendar workhorse — <b>days before or after a date field</b> (three days before a Purchase Order's expected date; on the compliance calendar's filing dates via their carrying doctype).<br>• <b>The condition</b> — the filter that scopes the rule (only submitted documents, only where a status field says so, only above a value). The condition discipline is the chapter's core caution: <b>an unscoped notification is spam at machine scale</b>, and spam teaches recipients to ignore the channel — after which the notification that matters dies in the same ignored folder.<br>• <b>The recipients</b> — by role, by a field on the document (the document's owner, the assigned user), or fixed addresses. Prefer roles and document fields to named people: the notification wired to a person breaks at their leaving; wired to a role it survives (module 2's access-follows-the-job, applied to mail).<br>• <b>The message</b> — subject and body, with the document's fields available as placeholders; the document link included so the recipient lands one click from action.</p><p><b>The notification register.</b> Every active Notification is a channel register line: purpose, owner, recipients, last reviewed. The annual review reads them all — the notification for the process that no longer exists is retired, and the one nobody remembers requesting gets the module 5 archaeology. The looping notification (a rule that fires on save and edits that trigger saves) is the classic incident; the queue's volume read (Chapter 5) is what catches it.</p><p><b>The Auto Email Report doctype.</b> Open <b>Auto Email Report</b>: a saved report, emailed on a schedule — daily, weekly, monthly — to named recipients, with filters fixed and the format chosen. This is the push layer over module 1's report machinery, and its lawful uses are the standing reads the tracks defined: the branch P&amp;L to each branch manager monthly, the aged receivables to the collections owner weekly, the draft-documents list to the close owner on day one.</p><p>The same two rules govern: <b>recipients by role/need</b> (the report to someone who never opens it is noise plus a data-exposure surface — the report email carries business data into mailboxes, so the recipient list is a permission decision), and <b>the register line</b> with an annual review that retires the unread.</p><p>Practical rule: the test for every automated message is the recipient's verdict, asked annually: <i>do you act on this?</i> Keep the ones that move work; retire the rest. A lean automated channel is one people actually read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Integrations and credential custody", "12", "<p>An integration is another system connected to the site — reading it, writing to it, or both. Each one is a door, and doors get the module's full treatment.</p><p><b>The standing example.</b> The e-invoicing rail: ZhiftERP transmits invoices to the tax authority through the <b>NGE Portal</b> integration (the tax module's compliance machinery). It carries every property this chapter governs: an external endpoint, a credential, a data flow of consequence, a failure mode (invoices not transmitting) that is silent locally and loud legally, and a health check the business must own. Payment gateways, SMS gateways (the <b>SMS Settings</b> page where the business sends transactional SMS), and any partner system connected by API follow the same pattern.</p><p><b>Credentials — the custody rules.</b> Integration credentials are keys to the business's data, and they concentrate the module's risk:</p><p>1. <b>Issued minimal:</b> where the platform supports API keys per user (the <b>API Access</b> section on the User record generates a key/secret pair), the integration gets its <b>own dedicated user</b> with exactly the roles its function needs (module 2's least-privilege applied to machines) — never a person's credentials, and never a System Manager's. The integration user is named for its function (<i>nge-portal-integration</i>), fenced like any user, and visible in every access review.<br>2. <b>Stored in custody:</b> the business's credential store — not chat history, not a spreadsheet, not one person's head. Access to the store: the sysadmin and backstop, listed.<br>3. <b>Rotated on the calendar and at events:</b> the security calendar's rotation line (module 8), plus immediate rotation when a holder leaves or exposure is suspected — and the rotation updates <b>both ends</b> (the store and the connected system's configuration), or Chapter 5's stuck-queue lesson repeats with a different channel.<br>4. <b>Revoked when the integration retires:</b> the dead integration's live credential is a door with no doorman; retirement is a checklist (credential revoked, user disabled, register entry closed).</p><p><b>The data-flow question, asked at connection time.</b> Before any integration connects: what data flows out, what flows in, and who approved that flow? The answer goes in the register entry — because \"what does this integration actually see?\" is a question the business will eventually be asked (by an auditor, a customer, or its own owner), and connection time is when the answer is cheap.</p><p>Practical rule: count the doors quarterly — the integration register's entries against the actual API-enabled users and connected apps on the site. A door not in the register is the finding; module 5's reconciliation logic, applied to connections.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The integration register & the HealthTrade case", "13", "<p><b>The integration register — the channel inventory's heaviest section.</b> Per integration, one entry:</p><p>1. <b>What and why</b> — the system, the business purpose, the data flow in both directions.<br>2. <b>The owner</b> — the person who answers \"do we still need this\" and receives its failures.<br>3. <b>The credential</b> — which dedicated user, where the secret lives, last rotated.<br>4. <b>The health check</b> — the specific read that proves it is working, and its cadence: for the e-invoicing rail, the transmitted-invoices confirmation the tax module's routine expects; for a gateway, the last successful transaction's recency.<br>5. <b>The failure mode and response</b> — what silence looks like, who is told, what the workaround is (the runbook's scenario 4 logic, per channel).</p><p>The monthly integration line (module 1's calendar) walks the register's health checks; the quarterly door count (Chapter 7) reconciles it against reality.</p><p><b>The HealthTrade case — the channels year.</b></p><p><b>The identity move (Chapter 4).</b> The ground: all system mail leaving from a personal free-mail address the old implementation era configured. The move runs as a project: the domain's mail authentication set up with the domain custodian, the Email Account record rebuilt on <i>accounts@</i> the business domain, Default Outgoing set, credential into custody, the identity test passed to three external providers — and the next monthly statement run lands in inboxes. Collections notices two replies in the first week from customers who <i>had never been receiving</i> the statements: the old identity had been decorating spam folders for months. The deliverability lesson writes itself into the register.</p><p><b>The stuck queue, closed properly (Chapter 5).</b> Module 1's month-three catch — the stuck email queue — gets its root cause on this module's procedure: Error rows naming authentication failure; the cause, a well-meant password rotation that never reached the Email Account record; the fix, the record updated, the queue drained, <b>41 queued documents</b> sent late-but-sent (the customer-facing rule), and the rotation procedure amended with its update-both-ends step. The incident log's closing line becomes the training example.</p><p><b>The reminder layer built (Chapter 6).</b> The compliance calendar's dates get their Notifications (days-before rules, recipients by role), the close owner gets the day-one draft-documents Auto Email Report, and the branch P&amp;L mails monthly to each manager. The first annual do-you-act-on-this review retires <b>5 of 17</b> automated messages — the lean channel principle applied.</p><p><b>The doors counted (Chapters 7-8).</b> The register is built: the NGE Portal e-invoicing integration (dedicated integration user, credential into custody, the transmitted-invoices health check joining the monthly line) and the SMS gateway (same treatment). The first quarterly door count finds one undocumented API key on a former consultant's user — revoked the same day, the user long disabled but the key never was; the leaver checklist gains its \"and API keys\" line. The MD's note: <i>the system's mouths and doors now have names on them.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "11", "<p>The channel layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the personal-mailbox identity.</b> Symptom: system mail from a free-mail or personal address; statements in spam folders; the sent history leaving with a person. Cause: the identity rule unknown at setup (Chapter 4). Fix: the domain identity project — authentication with the domain custodian, the Email Account rebuilt, custody, the identity test — and the deliverability payoff measured at the next statement run.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the silent queue.</b> Symptom: documents \"sent\" for weeks that never arrived; collections weakening; the discovery made by an annoyed customer. Cause: the queue never read (Chapter 5). Fix: the daily thirty-second glance, the stuck-queue procedure, and the volume read that confirms the big runs fired.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — notification spam.</b> Symptom: automated mail everyone ignores; the important alert dead in the same folder; a looping rule flooding the queue. Cause: unscoped conditions and unreviewed rules (Chapter 6). Fix: the condition discipline at creation, recipients by role, the register with its annual do-you-act-on-this review, and the queue's volume read as the loop alarm.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the hard-typed format.</b> Symptom: the invoice showing a bank account the business closed two years ago; identity details that survived three reprints of reality. Cause: business data typed into print formats instead of printing from records (Chapters 2-3). Fix: the rule — fields from the document, identity from the letterhead, nothing hard-typed — enforced by the specimen review, and the offending formats corrected through module 5's lane.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the credential in someone's head.</b> Symptom: the integration only one person can fix; the rotation that breaks the channel because only one end was updated; the leaver who takes the mail password with them. Cause: custody never established (Chapters 4, 7). Fix: the credential store with its access list, dedicated integration users at least privilege, rotation with the update-both-ends step, and the leaver checklist's credential lines.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the unregistered door.</b> Symptom: an API key nobody can explain; an integration discovered during an incident; the dead connection with the live credential. Cause: doors opened without register entries (Chapters 7-8). Fix: the register as the connection procedure's output, the quarterly door count reconciling register to reality, and the retirement checklist closing what is no longer needed.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the channel rule and keep the inventory; configure Letter Head and Print Settings and run the specimen discipline; govern Print Formats by the three levels and the assignment rules; set up the domain sending identity on the Email Account record and run the identity test; read the Email Queue's statuses and run the stuck-queue procedure; build scoped Notifications and Auto Email Reports with recipients by role and the annual review; apply the four credential custody rules with dedicated integration users; and keep the integration register with its health checks and the quarterly door count.</p><p><b>The sixth law: every channel has an owner, an identity the business controls, a credential under custody, and a health read on the calendar — silence is a finding, not a comfort.</b> Module 7 covers monitoring and the health reads.</p>"),
]

